module contract;

// W47-CONDVAR-JOIN-STOP-PATHS (gateway rule 10q as amended, contract
// specification §C04.10 and §C06.1). The engine thread's start, stop and the
// shutdown joins of the threads it starts, as observed in the bodies.
//
// ## Why every declaration here awaits review
//
// What these functions do to a thread -- start it, hand it `ctx`, join it,
// ignore the join's result, return without joining -- is not a resource-v1
// custody fact, and v1 has no clause for it. So each declaration is
// **withheld** (`resource_review_pending`, §C06.1): it loads, it is pinned, it
// discharges nothing and accuses nothing, every call that would apply it is
// held under `awaiting-human-review` (a verdict resting only on such holds is
// `Pending`, never `Safe`), and no consumer applies the custody clauses below
// while the marker stands. The reason strings carry the observations; they
// are data for the reviewer, not assumptions of any analysis.
//
// ## Which observations are checked, and against what
//
// * **Checked against the body by the rule 10q census** (the lexical path
//   reader over the `90978ac6` export, `docs/reports/w47-condvar-join-stop-paths/`):
//   `flb_stop`'s branches (the `!ctx->config` return derived infeasible under
//   P-DEREF; the status branch's unchecked join; the checked join returned),
//   `do_start`'s two unchecked joins on its failure returns, the repeated
//   join a failed `flb_start` followed by `flb_stop` performs, and
//   `mk_utils_worker_spawn`'s joinable creation.
// * **Read, not derived by the census**: the shutdown joins
//   (`flb_input_thread_instance_exit`, `flb_output_thread_pool_destroy`,
//   `flb_downstream_worker_runtime_stop`). The census leaves every
//   descendant of the engine thread unresolved: the engine thread's path from
//   its entry to its exit is not read.
// * **Body identity**: `quirin-audit pin` records each body's identity at the
//   revisions it was reviewed against (`contracts.lock`); a changed body puts
//   the declaration out of force until it is reviewed again.
//
// ## Custody clauses, pending with the rest
//
// `ctx` and `ins` are not freed by these calls (`flb_stop` releases
// `ctx->config->cf_main`, a member, and `flb_output_thread_pool_destroy`
// releases `ins->tp`); `flb_downstream_worker_runtime_stop` frees
// `runtime` except on its self-join refusal; the results are scalars.
// `flb_start` hands `ctx` to the engine thread until it is joined, which
// `resource_borrowed` does not say: that clause is exactly what a reviewer
// must settle.
//
// Nothing here is a verified race, a verified defect or an `Unsafe` claim. A
// "candidate" names an upstream path whose confirmation needs a native
// reproduction.

// ## The paired lifecycle (W47-FLB-LIB-LIFECYCLE, contract specification
// §C04.11, rule 10q as amended)
//
// `requires resource_lifecycle(ctx, start|stop|destroy)` states the library
// API as the user gave it (2026-09-29): `flb_start` and `flb_stop` come in
// pairs, a stop after the first has no effect, and a start without a stop in
// between is not permitted; this module's answer for what the statement
// leaves open is that a stop after a failed start and a stop of a
// never-started context have no effect either, and that `flb_destroy` is
// permitted only on a created or stopped context. The six clauses it implies
// are **specifications to check, never facts**: the rule 10q census runs the
// declared bodies from each clause's state and applies only a clause every
// followed path satisfies; a clause the bodies refute is withdrawn from every
// caller (rule 10g) and read from the body instead. No per-unit route derives
// a context's lifecycle state, so there every call of these three functions
// is refused (`outside its declared lifecycle obligation`), behind the review
// hold that applies first.
//
// Checked at the 90978ac6 export, 046accf6 and 66910c10
// (docs/reports/w47-flb-lib-lifecycle/): L1 (start only from created or
// stopped) and L6 (destroy only from created or stopped) are preconditions,
// in force as restrictions; `flb_destroy`'s own body never joins
// `ctx->config->worker`, so L6 is load-bearing. L2 (the first stop after a
// start that returned 0 joins the thread that start began, once) is
// satisfied by `flb_stop`'s body on every followed path, so it is in force;
// L3 (a stop
// after a stop has no effect), L4 (a stop after a failed start has no effect)
// and L5 (a stop of a never-started context has no effect) are **refuted by
// the body**: every feasible path of `flb_stop` joins `ctx->config->worker`
// (:1058 or :1077), which no path clears. Candidates, not verified defects
// (each needs a native reproduction): a second `flb_stop` joins the joined
// engine thread again; a stop after `do_start` joined on its failure return
// (:984, :999) joins it a second time; a stop after a start that failed
// before its spawn, or of a never-started context, joins an identifier no
// start produced.

extern fn flb_start(ctx: ptr<mut, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_lifecycle(ctx, start);
    requires resource_review_pending("Observed at 90978ac6 (src/flb_lib.c), checked by the rule 10q census: do_start spawns the engine thread flb_lib_worker through mk_utils_worker_spawn, which creates it joinable (PTHREAD_CREATE_JOINABLE, lib/monkey/mk_core/mk_utils.c:316-318), and stores its identifier in ctx->config->worker (:973). On both failure returns after the spawn (:980-986, :994-1001) it joins the thread with the result ignored and sets ctx->status to FLB_LIB_ERROR. It returns 0 without setting FLB_LIB_OK when the notification loop finds no started or failed value (:1008). ctx is handed to the engine thread until that thread is joined; v1 cannot state a thread start, a join or that retention.", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ctx);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_stop(ctx: ptr<mut, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_lifecycle(ctx, stop);
    requires resource_review_pending("Observed at 90978ac6 (src/flb_lib.c), checked by the rule 10q census: flb_stop reads ctx->config->worker (:1047) before its !ctx->config test (:1062), and ctx->config is stored only by flb_create before publication (:174), so the return 0 without a join at :1063 is derived infeasible under P-DEREF. On the FLB_LIB_NONE or FLB_LIB_ERROR status branch (:1049) it joins the engine thread and returns 0 with the join result ignored (:1058). Otherwise it signals the engine to exit and returns the join result (:1077-1083). Candidate, not a verified defect: after a flb_start whose engine thread already failed and was joined in do_start (:984, :999, status FLB_LIB_ERROR), the status branch joins that thread a second time (:1058), undefined under POSIX; confirming it needs a native reproduction. The status read (:1049) is not synchronised with the engine thread's own write (:932).", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ctx);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_destroy(ctx: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_lifecycle(ctx, destroy);
    requires resource_review_pending("Observed at 90978ac6 (src/flb_lib.c:230-263), identical at 046accf6 and 66910c10, read by the rule 10q census: flb_destroy returns at once on a null ctx; otherwise it deletes and frees ctx->event_channel, destroys ctx->event_loop, shuts the engine down when ctx->config->is_running is still true (flb_engine_shutdown) and releases ctx->config (flb_config_exit), then frees ctx. Its own body contains no join and never joins ctx->config->worker; the joins its callees reach are the output and input workers' and, through the chunk-trace pipeline (src/flb_chunk_trace.c:106, :270), another context's flb_stop, which the entry-keyed census cannot tell from this context's engine thread (P-JOIN-IDENTITY). So a destroy while this context's engine thread is live frees what that thread retains: the lifecycle clause L6 (destroy only from created or stopped) is a precondition on the caller, not an effect of the body. Whether the nullable release of ctx is the whole custody effect (the members it frees, the engine shutdown's own releases) is not derived here.", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_released(ctx, nullable);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_input_thread_instance_exit(ins: ptr<mut, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_review_pending("Read at 90978ac6 (src/flb_input_thread.c), not derived by the census: it copies the input worker's identifier (:591), writes the exit command to the worker and joins it with the result ignored (:607), returning 0. When the command cannot be written it returns -1 without joining (:602-604); whether a caller then releases what the worker retains is not derived.", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ins);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_output_thread_pool_destroy(ins: ptr<mut, u8>) -> unit
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_review_pending("Read at 90978ac6 (src/flb_output_thread.c), not derived by the census: for each running pool worker it writes the stop command and joins the worker with the result ignored (:646), then destroys the pool (:650). Candidate, not a verified defect: a worker whose stop command cannot be written is skipped without a join (:640-644) and its parameters are freed while it may still run; confirming it needs a native reproduction of the failed write.", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_borrowed(ins);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};

extern fn flb_downstream_worker_runtime_stop(runtime: ptr<mut, u8>) -> i32
contract {
    requires resource_contract_v1;
    requires resource_review_pending("Read at 90978ac6 (src/flb_downstream_worker.c), not derived by the census: called from one of its own workers it returns -1 without joining (:491-492, which avoids a self join) and without freeing the runtime; otherwise it waits for active operations under the lifecycle mutex, wakes every worker and joins each created worker with the result ignored (:520) before destroying the runtime's synchronisation objects and freeing it. The runtime is freed on that path and not on the -1 return, which v1 cannot state per path.", "qcore-c integrator");
    ensures normal resource_released(runtime, nullable);
    ensures normal resource_return_none();
};
